{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-104586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=5{t}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(5{t}^{2}){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}+(5{t}^{2}){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}+(5{t}^{2}){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+0+10 \\\\  & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{-1}+(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{0}+(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(t\\,+\\,1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot (t+1) \\\\  & =2(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-104586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(t+1)^{2}"] ["math" "2(t+1)"] ["math" "2t+1"] ["math" "3t+1"] ["math" "t+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2(t+1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=2{t}^{2}-2t$ onto the subspace spanned by $q(t)=2t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{-1}+(2{t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{0}+(2{t}^{2}\\,-\\,2t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8+0+0 \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(2t){|}_{-1}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{-1}+(2t){|}_{0}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{0}+(2t){|}_{1}\\,\\cdot \\,(2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4+0+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{8}\\cdot (2t) \\\\  & =-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-104862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2t"] ["math" "-{t}^{2}"] ["math" "2t-1"] ["math" "4t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=4{t}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=6t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{2}\\cdot 6t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}24{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[6{t}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}36{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{12}\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-104583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12t"] ["math" "3t"] ["math" "4t"] ["math" "6t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=12({t}^{2}-t)$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2}+12t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[3{t}^{4}-8{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-8+6)-0 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104587" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-104587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2(t-1)"] ["math" "3(t-1)"] ["math" "3t-1"] ["math" "t-3"] ["math" "{t}^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104587" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3(t-1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=16{t}^{3}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=3{t}^{2}-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{3}+2)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(48{t}^{5}-16{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[8{t}^{6}-4{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}-2t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(8-4+2-2)-0 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2}-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{4}-6{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}-2{t}^{3}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{5}-2+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{4}{5})}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =5(3{t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3{t}^{2}"] ["math" "-5(3t-1)"] ["math" "5(3{t}^{2}-1)"] ["math" "9(3{t}^{2}-1)"] ["math" "{t}^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5(3{t}^{2}-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$ \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,g(t)=15t+3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $15t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=15t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(15t+3)\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}15{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{15}{4}{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{15}{4}+1)-(\\frac{15}{4}-1) \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{f}_{2}(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{5}{t}^{5}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-(-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{2}{(\\frac{2}{5})}\\cdot {t}^{2}=5{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =15t+5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-105400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "15t+3{t}^{2}"] ["math" "15t+5{t}^{2}"] ["math" "3t+5{t}^{2}"] ["math" "3{t}^{2}"] ["math" "5t+3{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15t+5{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $1,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=5t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-2t+2,\\,q(t)=-t-4\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}$ equals $-5t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=-5t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(-t-4){|}_{0}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{0}+(-t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(-2t+2)^{2}{|}_{0}+(-2t+2)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{-8}{4}\\cdot (-2t+2)=4t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =-5t+(4t-4) \\\\  & =-t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4t+1"] ["math" "-t-4"] ["math" "4t-1"] ["math" "t+4"] ["math" "t-4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-t-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,g(t)={sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}$ equals $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{1}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while ${sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105416" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-105416" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105416" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105416" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-105136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105136" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,g(t)=\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $3t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=3t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while ${t}^{2}$ is even. Hence, their product ${t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot {t}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =3t+0 \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{t}^{2}+3t"] ["math" "3t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t"] ["math" "\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+t"] ["math" "t\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=2t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=6t-3$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}2t\\cdot (6t-3)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{2}-6t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{[4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}}{{[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]}_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-3)-0}{(12-18+9)-0}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=2t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=6t-3$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖2t-(2t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖1‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨1,1⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{1}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-(-3t+3)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-3‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-18+9)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=5t$ and $g(t)=7t-8$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The distance between $f(t)=5t$ and $g(t)=7t-8$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖5t-(7t-8)‖ \\\\  & =‖-2t+8‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨-2t+8,-2t+8⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2t+8,-2t+8⟩ & =(-2t+8){|}_{0}\\cdot (-2t+8){|}_{0}+(-2t+8){|}_{1}\\cdot (-2t+8){|}_{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 8+6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{100}=10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-105492" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105492" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{15}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105492" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-98751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=6t$ and $g(t)=5{t}^{2}$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-98751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The distance between $f(t)=6t$ and $g(t)=5{t}^{2}$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-5{t}^{2}‖ \\\\  & =‖5{t}^{2}-6t‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨5{t}^{2}-6t,5{t}^{2}-6t⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨5{t}^{2}-6t,5{t}^{2}-6t⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5{t}^{2}-6t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(25{t}^{4}-60{t}^{3}+36{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[5{t}^{5}-15{t}^{4}+12{t}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(5-15+12)-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-98751" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-98751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-98751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{6}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-98751" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=7{t}^{2}-t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=4{t}^{2}-4t$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(7{t}^{2}-t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 0}{8\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{64}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=7{t}^{2}-t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=4{t}^{2}-4t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖7{t}^{2}-t-(4{t}^{2}-4t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3{t}^{2}+3t‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3{t}^{2}+3t,3{t}^{2}+3t⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3{t}^{2}+3t,3{t}^{2}+3t⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(3{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0+0+36 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{36}=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "23910cb61c8a990c7e2cb3c14e6f0e2465f4f0d7956f17093018d297cdd294f0"} {"question" "e-8729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=-5t-3$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t) =t-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8729.json" "file_sha256" "54fc162f1db27b39c5541e140bf8db32732bbfb79825fc8d07fe011f6ca3396a"} {"question" "e-8729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{p(t),q(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{q(t),q(t)}\\rangle}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(-5t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(-5t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-8)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3+0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8729.json" "file_sha256" "54fc162f1db27b39c5541e140bf8db32732bbfb79825fc8d07fe011f6ca3396a"} {"question" "e-8730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle =\\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf_1(t)=1,\\quad f_2(t)=\\sin \\pi t, \\quad g(t)=6t^2\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\}$ given that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}$ equals $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8730.json" "file_sha256" "59c9fbd969747e3bba1ff73978987beaf937af5047cd8246bd936d2c83ad3bb5"} {"question" "e-8730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,g(t) \n= \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) = 2,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{g(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}\\,f_2(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sin (\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while $6t^2$ is even. Hence, their product $6 t^2 \\sin (\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1}   6t^2 \\sin (\\pi t) \\, \\text{d}t = 0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{0}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle} \\cdot  \\sin \\pi t = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8730.json" "file_sha256" "59c9fbd969747e3bba1ff73978987beaf937af5047cd8246bd936d2c83ad3bb5"} {"question" "e-8733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8733.json" "file_sha256" "2c9df2ab7c7e01fb80d84eae3996f692eda064012ec6a9c86148ae59f85c18fa"} {"question" "e-8733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(0)^{2}+q(1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(0)+(6)(1)}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot t \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖(4t+2)-6t‖ \\\\  & =‖-2t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩ & =(-2t+2){|}_{0}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{0}+(-2t+2){|}_{1}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-2(0)+2)^{2}+(-2(1)+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d = \\sqrt{4} = 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8733.json" "file_sha256" "2c9df2ab7c7e01fb80d84eae3996f692eda064012ec6a9c86148ae59f85c18fa"}] "usage" {"e-8733" [] "q-98750" [] "e-8730" [] "q-98751" [] "q-105416" [] "e-8729" []} "basis" 1759 "retractions" [[17592186133986 :question/problem "Let $V = C[- π, π]$, the vector space of continuous functions over $[- π, π]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = ∫_{- π}^{π}x(t)y(t)dt$.\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V$:\n\n$f_{1}(t) = \\sin t, f_{2}(t) = \\cos t, g(t) = \\sin^{2} t - \\cos t$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S = Span{f_{1}(t), f_{2}(t)}$ given that $f_{1}(t)⊥f_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $Span{f_{2}(t)}$ equals $-\\cos t$."] [17592186134484 :question/problem "Let $V = C[0, 1]$, the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0, 1]$. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = ∫_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)dt$,\nfind the distance between $f(t) = 2t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t) = 6t - 3$ with respect to the inner product."] [17592186134485 :question/problem "Let $V = C[0, 1]$, the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0, 1]$. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = ∫_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)dt$,\nfind the distance between $f(t) = 6t$ and $g(t) = 5t^{2}$ with respect to the inner product."] [17592186133612 :question/problem "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t]$, the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n$\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)$,\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=-5t-3$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t) =t-1$."] [17592186133612 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) = \\dfrac{\\langle{p(t),q(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{q(t),q(t)}\\rangle}\\,q(t)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨p(t), q(t)⟩ &= p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1) \\\\\n&= (-5t - 3)\\begin{vmatrix}_{0} \\cdot (t - 1) & _{0} + (-5t - 3) & _{1} \\cdot (t - 1)\\end{vmatrix}_{1} \\\\\n&= (-3) \\cdot (-1) + (-8) \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 3 + 0 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨q(t), q(t)⟩ &= q(0)q(0) + q(1)q(1) \\\\\n&= (t - 1)\\begin{vmatrix}_{0} \\cdot (t - 1) & _{0} + (t - 1) & _{1} \\cdot (t - 1)\\end{vmatrix}_{1} \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot (-1) + 0 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{q(t)}p(t) &= (⟨p(t), q(t)⟩)/(⟨q(t), q(t)⟩)q(t) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{1} \\cdot (t - 1) \\\\\n&= 3(t - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133613 :question/problem "Let $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$, the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle =\\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t$.\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V$:\n\n$f_1(t)=1,\\quad f_2(t)=\\sin \\pi t, \\quad g(t)=6t^2$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\}$ given that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}$ equals $2$."] [17592186133613 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,g(t) = \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t)\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) = 2\n$$\n\nso let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}$. This can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{\\langle{g(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}\\,f_2(t)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨g(t), f_{2}(t)⟩ &= ∫_{-1}^{1}g(t)f_{2}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}6t^{2} \\cdot \\sin (πt)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}6t^{2}\\sin (πt)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sin (\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while $6t^2$ is even. Hence, their product $6 t^2 \\sin (\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1]$, which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} 6t^2 \\sin (\\pi t) \\, \\text{d}t = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{0}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle} \\cdot \\sin \\pi t = 0\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}g(t) &= proj_{f_{1}(t)}g(t) + proj_{f_{2}(t)}g(t) \\\\\n&= 2 + 0 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133614 :question/problem "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t]$, the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n$\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1)$,\nfind the distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to the inner product."] [17592186133614 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{q(t)}p(t) &= (⟨p(t), q(t)⟩)/(⟨q(t), q(t)⟩)q(t) \\\\\n&= (p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1))/(q(0)^{2} + q(1)^{2}) \\cdot q(t) \\\\\n&= ((2)(0) + (6)(1))/(0^{2} + 1^{2}) \\cdot t \\\\\n&= 6t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between\n\n$$\np(t) = 4t + 2\n$$\n\nand the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= ‖p(t) - proj_{q(t)}p(t)‖ \\\\\n&= ‖(4t + 2) - 6t‖ \\\\\n&= ‖ - 2t + 2‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{⟨ - 2t + 2,-2t + 2⟩}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨ - 2t + 2,-2t + 2⟩ &= (-2t + 2)\\begin{vmatrix}_{0} \\cdot (-2t + 2) & _{0} + (-2t + 2) & _{1} \\cdot (-2t + 2)\\end{vmatrix}_{1} \\\\\n&= (-2(0) + 2)^{2} + (-2(1) + 2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} + 0^{2} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186130480 17592186130481 17592186130482 17592186130483 17592186130484 17592186133347 17592186133348 17592186133349 17592186133350 17592186133351 17592186133353 17592186133354 17592186133355 17592186133356 17592186133357] "immutable_fields" [[17592186130479 :answer-field/correct] [17592186130479 :answer-field/id] [17592186130479 :answer-field/key] [17592186130479 :answer-field/type] [17592186133346 :answer-field/correct] [17592186133346 :answer-field/id] [17592186133346 :answer-field/key] [17592186133346 :answer-field/type] [17592186133352 :answer-field/correct] [17592186133352 :answer-field/id] [17592186133352 :answer-field/key] [17592186133352 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
